.TH ASINH 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
asinh, asinhf, asinhl, acosh, acoshf, acoshl, atanh, atanhf, atanhl \- inverse hyperbolic functions
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double asinh(double " x );
.br
.BI "float asinhf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double asinhl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double acosh(double " x );
.br
.BI "float acoshf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double acoshl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double atanh(double " x );
.br
.BI "float atanhf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double atanhl(long double " x );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR asinh ()
function returns the inverse hyperbolic sine of
.IR x ,
defined mathematically as
.RI log( x \ +\ \[sr]( x^2 \ +\ 1)).
It is an odd function whose domain is the entire real line and whose
range is also the entire real line.
.PP
The
.BR acosh ()
function returns the inverse hyperbolic cosine of
.IR x ,
defined mathematically as
.RI log( x \ +\ \[sr]( x^2 \ \-\ 1)).
Its domain is the half-open interval
.BR [1,\ +\[if]) ,
and its range is the half-open interval
.BR [0,\ +\[if]) .
.PP
The
.BR atanh ()
function returns the inverse hyperbolic tangent of
.IR x ,
defined mathematically as
.RI (1/2)\ log((1\ +\ x )/(1\ \-\ x )).
It is an odd function whose domain is the open interval
.BR (\-1,\ +1)
and whose range is the entire real line, with vertical asymptotes
at \(+-1.
.PP
The
.BR asinhf (),
.BR acoshf (),
and
.BR atanhf ()
variants take and return
.B float
values.  The
.BR asinhl (),
.BR acoshl (),
and
.BR atanhl ()
variants take and return
.B "long double"
values.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, these functions return the inverse hyperbolic value of
.I x
as described above.
.PP
If
.I x
is +0 (\-0),
.BR asinh ()
and
.BR atanh ()
return +0 (\-0).
.BR acosh (1)
returns +0.
.PP
If
.I x
is positive infinity,
.BR asinh ()
returns positive infinity and
.BR acosh ()
returns positive infinity.  If
.I x
is negative infinity,
.BR asinh ()
returns negative infinity.
.PP
If
.I x
is a NaN, all three functions return a NaN.
.PP
For
.BR acosh (),
if
.I x
is less than 1, a domain error occurs and the function returns a NaN.
.PP
For
.BR atanh (),
if
.I x
is \(+-1, a pole error occurs and the function returns
.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
(or
.B \(+-HUGE_VALF
or
.BR \(+-HUGE_VALL )
with the appropriate sign.  If the magnitude of
.I x
is greater than 1, a domain error occurs and the function returns a
NaN.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Domain error: \fIx\fR < 1 (\fBacosh\fR)
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.TP
Domain error: |\fIx\fR| > 1 (\fBatanh\fR)
An invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.TP
Pole error: \fIx\fR is \(+-1 (\fBatanh\fR)
A divide-by-zero floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_DIVBYZERO )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.PP
.BR asinh ()
does not raise errors for any finite or infinite argument.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR asinh (),
.BR asinhf (),
.BR asinhl (),
.BR acosh (),
.BR acoshf (),
.BR acoshl (),
.BR atanh (),
.BR atanhf (),
.BR atanhl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR asinh (),
.BR acosh (),
.BR atanh (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.  The handling of special
arguments follows C99 Annex F.10.3 (IEC 60559).
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program computes the inverse hyperbolic sine, cosine,
and tangent for representative arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    printf("asinh(%.6f) = %.6f\en", 1.0, asinh(1.0));
    printf("acosh(%.6f) = %.6f\en", 1.0, acosh(1.0));
    printf("atanh(%.6f) = %.6f\en", 0.5, atanh(0.5));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
